## Domain 1: Agents and Workflows (Q1.1–Q1.9)

### Q1.1 · Agent Architecture
A trip-booking assistant walks through fixed steps for every request — collect dates,
collect destination, check availability, confirm price — but occasionally a user asks
for a multi-city itinerary with flexible dates, which requires genuinely exploring
several combinations and comparing tradeoffs before anything can be confirmed.

A) A pure fixed workflow for every request, since the majority of bookings are simple
   and the multi-city cases can be told to simplify their request instead.
B) A hybrid: the fixed sequence handles simple single-destination bookings end to end,
   with a branch to agentic, exploratory reasoning specifically for the multi-city/
   flexible-date case that a fixed sequence can't represent in advance.
C) A pure autonomous agent for every booking request, since any request could in
   principle turn out to need multi-city exploration.
D) A supervisor with a dedicated subagent per possible destination, since decomposing
   by destination always improves reliability regardless of request complexity.

### Q1.2 · Agent Architecture
A supervisor agent sits in front of three specialists — billing, shipping, returns —
but its routing logic always forwards every request to the billing specialist first
"to be thorough," even for requests that are unambiguously about a return with no
billing dimension at all.

A) This is correct supervisor behavior, since routing every request through every
   specialist in some order guarantees nothing is missed.
B) This defeats the purpose of having specialists: the supervisor should classify the
   request and route to the specialist(s) the request actually concerns, not forward
   everything through one specialist by default regardless of relevance.
C) This is correct as long as the billing specialist is the fastest of the three, since
   starting with the fastest specialist minimizes total latency.
D) This is a Claude Hooks problem, since routing logic should be enforced by a
   `PreToolUse` hook rather than the supervisor's own reasoning.

### Q1.3 · Agent Architecture
A debugging assistant is asked to (1) report how many open GitHub issues a repo
currently has, and (2) reproduce a flaky test failure by trying several different
environment configurations until one triggers it. The team implements both as plain
sequential tool calls in the main agent's own context.

A) Both are implemented correctly, since subagent delegation is only relevant when
   multiple different tools are involved, and both tasks here use similarly few tools.
B) The issue count is fine as a single tool call in the main context; the flaky-test
   reproduction is the kind of open-ended, exploratory, potentially context-heavy work
   that benefits from subagent delegation, since it can churn through many attempts
   without polluting the main session.
C) Both should be delegated to subagents, since any task requiring more than one tool
   call in sequence should always be isolated in its own context.
D) Neither should be delegated, since subagents are reserved exclusively for tasks
   involving multiple specialist domains, not exploratory single-domain work.

### Q1.4 · Agent Construction with Claude
An agent on the Claude Agent SDK is bounded only by `max_budget_usd`. In production, a
bug causes it to make many extremely cheap, low-token tool calls in a tight loop —
thousands of turns — that individually cost almost nothing, so the dollar budget is
never tripped even though the agent has been looping for far longer than any
reasonable task should take.

A) `max_budget_usd` alone is sufficient here, since a runaway loop of cheap calls that
   never exceeds the budget isn't actually a problem worth bounding separately.
B) `max_budget_usd` and `max_turns` guard against different failure modes — a budget
   cap doesn't help when each turn is individually cheap but the turn count runs away;
   `max_turns` should be set alongside the budget cap to bound iteration count
   independent of per-turn cost.
C) The fix is to raise `max_budget_usd` further, since a higher budget threshold will
   eventually be tripped and terminate the loop.
D) The fix is to lower `max_tokens` per turn, since smaller individual responses will
   cause the loop to terminate on its own once output becomes too short to be useful.

### Q1.5 · Agent Construction with Claude
Two engineers are working from the same active coding session. One wants to try a
risky, experimental refactor without touching or being affected by the main thread's
ongoing work; the other wants to keep working on the main thread exactly as it was,
unaffected by whatever the experimental attempt does.

A) `context: fork` for the experimental refactor, branching an isolated copy of the
   current context to explore in without affecting the main thread, which continues
   independently.
B) Session resume for the experimental refactor, since resuming from a saved state is
   the correct way to create an isolated branch for risky exploratory work.
C) Both engineers should work in the exact same unforked session, since Claude Code
   sessions are inherently safe for concurrent divergent editing without any isolation
   mechanism.
D) `context: fork` for the main thread instead of the experimental work, since the
   thread that should stay unaffected is the one that needs to be forked away from
   risk.

### Q1.6 · Agent Construction with Claude
A retailer expects extreme, unpredictable traffic spikes during a small number of peak
sales events each year, with normal traffic the rest of the time. They have no desire
to provision and manage infrastructure sized for peak load year-round, and want to add
or reduce capacity for an agent-powered shopping assistant with minimal lead time.

A) A self-hosted deployment sized for peak load year-round, since having dedicated
   infrastructure always available is the safest way to guarantee capacity during a
   spike.
B) An Anthropic-hosted managed deployment, which lets the team absorb elastic,
   unpredictable demand without provisioning and maintaining infrastructure sized for
   rare peak events — trading some low-level infrastructure control for not having to
   manage that capacity planning themselves.
C) A self-hosted deployment is required regardless of traffic pattern, since managed
   deployments cannot handle any variability in request volume.
D) Neither deployment model can handle unpredictable spiky traffic; this scenario
   requires a custom-built infrastructure layer outside either standard option.

### Q1.7 · Agent Patterns and Frameworks
A multi-step data-processing agent must produce strictly validated, typed outputs at
every intermediate step, since a malformed intermediate value would silently corrupt
everything downstream. A separate, much simpler agent just answers one-off questions
with free-form text and has no structured intermediate steps at all.

A) A framework emphasizing strict typed validation at each step (e.g., a
   typed-output-oriented framework) is a reasonable fit for the data-processing
   agent's genuine correctness need; imposing that same structure on the simple
   free-form Q&A agent would add validation machinery the task doesn't need.
B) Both agents should use the same typed-validation framework uniformly, since strict
   validation is never a disadvantage regardless of a task's actual structure.
C) Neither agent benefits from any framework, since typed validation is something only
   custom-written code can provide.
D) The simple Q&A agent needs the typed framework more, since free-form text output is
   inherently less reliable than structured intermediate steps and needs more
   validation, not less.

### Q1.8 · Agent Patterns and Frameworks
A support bot handles an escalated case that can remain open for several days,
sometimes spanning multiple separate conversations as different agents or the same
customer check back in. The team currently keeps case details only in the active
session's context, so anything relevant is lost the moment that particular session
ends.

A) This is fine, since case-relevant details naturally belong in session context and
   don't need any dedicated persistence mechanism.
B) Case details should be persisted in memory keyed to the case/ticket itself (not just
   the session or even the user), and loaded into context whenever any session —
   regardless of which conversation or which specific agent — picks the case back up.
C) Case details should be persisted keyed to the specific user only, since a case is
   always associated with exactly one person regardless of how many separate
   conversations touch it.
D) Increasing the context window is the correct fix, since a large enough window would
   let the original session simply stay open indefinitely across days.

### Q1.9 · Agent Patterns and Frameworks *(select TWO)*
Which two statements accurately describe genuine tradeoffs of a manager/supervisor
multi-agent pattern, as opposed to a single generalist agent?

A) A supervisor pattern can improve reliability on genuinely multi-domain requests by
   routing each sub-task to a specialist suited for it, rather than asking one
   generalist to handle every domain equally well.
B) A supervisor pattern eliminates coordination overhead entirely, since delegating to
   specialists is always cheaper and faster than handling a request in a single agent.
C) A supervisor pattern introduces real coordination costs — decomposing the request,
   routing correctly, and aggregating results coherently — that a single-agent
   approach doesn't have to manage at all.
D) A supervisor pattern guarantees that no request can ever be misrouted, since
   specialists are inherently more accurate than generalists at every task.
E) A supervisor pattern's aggregation step adds a place where individually-correct
   specialist outputs can still be combined incorrectly, a failure mode a single-agent
   approach doesn't have in the same form.

