## Domain 2: Applications and Integration (Q2.1–Q2.19)

### Q2.1 · Understanding Requirements
A requirements document for a financial-services assistant states: "the system shall
calculate a customer's loan eligibility score." A separate line states: "the system
must maintain 99.95% uptime, including automatic failover to a secondary region within
60 seconds of a primary region outage."

A) Both are functional requirements, since both describe capabilities the system must
   deliver.
B) The first is a functional requirement (a specific calculation behavior); the second
   is an infrastructure requirement — a disaster-recovery/availability constraint the
   implementation must satisfy regardless of which specific feature is running.
C) The second is a business goal rather than a requirement, since uptime targets
   ultimately serve customer trust and revenue.
D) Both are infrastructure requirements, since both are ultimately implemented as part
   of the same deployed system.

### Q2.2 · Understanding Requirements
A stakeholder's requirement reads: "the assistant should respond quickly and feel
snappy to users." An engineer proceeds to build against this requirement as written,
without seeking further clarification.

A) This is an adequately specified requirement and can be built against directly,
   since "quickly" and "snappy" convey enough information to guide implementation
   decisions.
B) This requirement is not yet testable as written — it lacks a measurable target
   (e.g., a specific latency threshold at a specific percentile); a well-formed
   requirement should be clarified into something concrete and verifiable before
   implementation proceeds against it.
C) This should be treated as an infrastructure requirement exactly as worded, without
   further refinement, since perceived speed is inherently a non-functional concern.
D) This requirement should be discarded entirely, since subjective language can never
   be turned into anything actionable.

### Q2.3 · Systems Life Cycle
A team has a finished technical design for a new agent — architecture, tool set, and
model selection all decided — and is now writing the actual code: implementing tools,
wiring up the agentic loop, and writing unit tests for individual components as they're
built.

A) This is system design, since writing code is simply the mechanical execution of
   decisions already made during design.
B) This is implementation — translating an already-completed design into working code,
   including the component-level tests written alongside it, is the defining activity
   of this phase, distinct from the design that preceded it.
C) This is requirements analysis, since unit tests are technically a form of
   specifying expected behavior.
D) This is operations and maintenance, since any code-writing activity that happens
   after initial requirements gathering falls under ongoing maintenance.

### Q2.4 · Systems Life Cycle
Before launch, a team runs its full eval suite against the finished implementation,
conducts user acceptance testing with a pilot group, and fixes issues found before the
system goes live to all users — no code has yet been exposed to real production
traffic.

A) This is operations and maintenance, since evaluating a system's behavior is
   inherently an ongoing, post-launch activity regardless of whether real users have
   seen it yet.
B) This is testing and validation — verifying the completed implementation against
   requirements and expected behavior before committing to a live release is a
   distinct life-cycle activity that comes after implementation and before the system
   is exposed to production traffic.
C) This is implementation, since fixing issues found during evaluation is technically
   still writing code.
D) This step is unnecessary and can be skipped whenever the eval suite was already run
   once during development.

### Q2.5 · Claude API Mechanics
A document-analysis feature needs to compare four different scanned pages of the same
multi-page form within a single request, cross-referencing values that appear on
different pages, and the team is estimating the token cost of supplying all four as
image content blocks in one message.

A) Multiple images can be supplied as multiple image content blocks within a single
   message; each image contributes to the input token count based on its
   resolution/size, so the team should account for all four images' token contribution
   together when estimating cost for the combined request, not just one representative
   image.
B) Only one image can be supplied per API request, so the four pages must be submitted
   as four entirely separate requests with no ability to cross-reference values across
   them in a single call.
C) Images don't count toward the token budget at all, so the number of images supplied
   has no bearing on cost or context window usage.
D) Supplying more than one image in a single request is only possible if extended
   thinking is disabled for that request.

### Q2.6 · Claude API Mechanics
A team enables extended thinking on a reasoning-heavy workload and is surprised to see
their token-based billing increase substantially beyond what they estimated from only
the visible final answer's length, since they assumed billing was based solely on the
visible response text.

A) This indicates a billing error, since only the visible final answer should ever
   count toward token-based costs regardless of any internal reasoning the model
   performs.
B) Thinking tokens are counted as part of the billed output, in addition to the final
   visible response — a workload that generates substantial internal reasoning before
   answering will show correspondingly higher output token counts than the visible
   answer length alone would suggest.
C) Extended thinking is always billed as a fixed flat fee per request regardless of how
   much reasoning is actually generated internally.
D) Thinking tokens only count toward cost when `effort` is explicitly set to its
   maximum level; at any lower setting, thinking is entirely free.

### Q2.7 · Claude API Mechanics
A streaming integration needs to execute a tool call as soon as the model decides to
make one, but the current implementation attempts to parse the `tool_use` block's
input JSON incrementally as partial fragments arrive via streaming events,
occasionally executing the tool against incomplete, invalid JSON before the block has
actually finished streaming.

A) This is expected and unavoidable; tool calls simply cannot be used reliably together
   with streaming under any implementation.
B) The tool's input JSON should be fully assembled from the accumulated streaming
   events (i.e., wait until the relevant content block is complete) before being
   parsed and executed — parsing or acting on a `tool_use` block's input before it has
   finished streaming risks operating on incomplete, invalid data.
C) The fix is to disable streaming entirely for any request that might result in a
   tool call, foregoing the responsiveness benefits of streaming for all tool-using
   requests.
D) The fix is to increase `max_tokens`, since incomplete JSON is typically a symptom of
   an output-length limit being hit rather than of reading a block before it's
   finished streaming.

### Q2.8 · Claude API Mechanics
A team wants Claude to reliably maintain a persistent persona and behavioral rules
across an entire conversation and puts those instructions into the first message in
the `messages` array with `role: "user"`, reasoning "the API reads the whole array
anyway, so placement inside `messages` versus the dedicated `system` parameter
shouldn't matter."

A) Placement matters: the dedicated `system` parameter is specifically designed to
   carry persistent, session-level instructions with a distinct role in how the model
   treats them, separate from the turn-by-turn conversational exchange the `messages`
   array represents — using a user-role message for this purpose is a different, less
   appropriate mechanism than the one built for it.
B) There is no meaningful difference; a user-role message containing persona
   instructions is functionally identical to the same content passed via the `system`
   parameter in every respect.
C) The `system` parameter is deprecated in favor of putting all instructions inside the
   first user message, so the team's current approach is actually the recommended one.
D) The `messages` array cannot contain a `role: "user"` entry as the very first message
   under any circumstances, so this configuration would be rejected outright by the
   API.

### Q2.9 · Software Engineering Foundations
A `create_support_ticket` tool call times out on the client side after being sent, and
the client's retry logic resubmits the identical request, unaware that the original
call may have already succeeded server-side — resulting in duplicate tickets being
created for the same issue on a meaningful fraction of timeouts.

A) This is acceptable since duplicate tickets are a minor cosmetic issue that doesn't
   warrant additional engineering effort to prevent.
B) The retry should be made safe via an idempotency mechanism (e.g., an idempotency key
   tied to the original request) so a retried call after a timeout is recognized as the
   same operation rather than creating a second ticket, rather than assuming every
   retry represents a request that never went through.
C) The fix is to remove retry logic entirely, accepting that any timed-out ticket
   creation is simply lost and must be manually re-created by the user.
D) The fix is to increase the client-side timeout duration, which fully eliminates the
   possibility of a timeout occurring under any load condition.

### Q2.10 · Software Engineering Foundations
A production Claude-integration service logs only "request failed" with no request ID,
no prompt reference, no model version, and no error detail, making it extremely
difficult to trace a specific user's reported issue back to what actually happened in
a given call.

A) This level of logging is sufficient, since the goal of logging is simply to record
   that a failure category occurred, not to enable tracing any specific instance of it.
B) Structured logging — capturing enough context per call (request/trace ID, model
   version, relevant prompt/response identifiers, and specific error detail) to
   reconstruct what happened for a given failure — is foundational to being able to
   debug production issues at all; sparse, unstructured logs make root-causing a
   specific incident far harder than it needs to be.
C) The correct fix is to upgrade to a larger, more capable model, since better model
   reasoning would reduce the underlying failure rate and therefore the need for
   detailed logs.
D) Logging detail is irrelevant as long as the team has an eval suite, since evals
   catch every failure mode logs would otherwise reveal.

### Q2.11 · Software Engineering Foundations
A team's test suite for a Claude-integrated feature consists entirely of end-to-end
integration tests that make real API calls, take minutes to run, and cost real money
on every CI run. There are no unit tests for the surrounding parsing, validation, or
business logic that doesn't actually need a live model call to verify.

A) This is optimal, since only end-to-end tests can meaningfully verify a Claude
   integration, making unit tests unnecessary regardless of what they'd cover.
B) A more balanced test strategy — fast, cheap unit tests for logic that doesn't need
   a live model call (parsing, validation, schema checks), reserving slower, costlier
   end-to-end tests for what genuinely requires exercising the real integration — would
   catch most regressions faster and cheaper than relying on integration tests alone.
C) The fix is to remove all integration tests and rely exclusively on unit tests, since
   unit tests are strictly better for any codebase.
D) Test strategy has no bearing on a Claude-integrated feature specifically, since
   testing practices for LLM-integrated code are identical to testing practices for
   any other code with no adjustments needed.

### Q2.12 · Software Engineering Foundations
A service pins its application dependencies but leaves the Anthropic SDK version
unconstrained ("always install latest"), reasoning that "staying current on the SDK is
always beneficial." A routine deploy pulls in a new SDK major version that changes a
default parameter behavior, silently altering production output.

A) This is expected and desirable, since SDK updates should always be adopted
   immediately and automatically regardless of what they change.
B) The SDK should be version-pinned like other dependencies, with upgrades treated as
   deliberate, tested changes rather than something that happens automatically on a
   routine deploy — an unconstrained "latest" dependency can introduce breaking
   behavior changes with no warning, just as an unconstrained "latest" model alias can.
C) This is unrelated to dependency management, since SDK behavior changes are
   considered model behavior changes and belong entirely under model version pinning
   instead.
D) The correct fix is to stop using the official SDK entirely and hand-roll HTTP calls,
   since only custom code can be fully protected from unannounced changes.

### Q2.13 · Claude Application Design
A team maintains one very long, undifferentiated system prompt covering persona,
tone, tool usage instructions, compliance rules, and formatting requirements — one
continuous block of text with no internal structure — and finds that updating any one
concern (e.g., just the compliance rules) requires carefully re-reading the entire
block to make sure the edit doesn't conflict with something else in it.

A) This is unavoidable, since a system prompt is fundamentally limited to being one
   undifferentiated block of text regardless of how it's authored or maintained.
B) Structuring and modularizing the prompt — clearly delineated sections, or separately
   maintained and composed fragments for persona, tools, compliance, and format — makes
   isolated updates safer and easier to reason about than one large undifferentiated
   block, even though the final assembled prompt sent to the model can still be one
   piece of text.
C) The fix is to remove all instructions except persona and tone, since compliance
   rules and formatting requirements don't belong in a system prompt at all.
D) The fix is to split the single system prompt into five entirely separate API calls,
   one per concern, since the API only accepts one instruction category per request.

### Q2.14 · Claude Application Design
When a Claude call fails or times out, a customer-facing application currently
surfaces the raw exception message and stack trace directly in the UI, verbatim,
including internal service names and file paths.

A) This is acceptable, since showing the exact underlying error is always more helpful
   to the end user than any alternative.
B) User-facing error handling should distinguish what a customer needs to see (a clear,
   actionable, non-technical message, possibly with a retry option) from what
   engineering needs for debugging (the full internal detail, which belongs in logs
   and internal monitoring, not the customer-facing surface).
C) The fix is to show no error indication at all when a call fails, leaving the UI in
   whatever state it was in before the failed call, since any error message risks
   confusing the user.
D) Raw internal errors are only a concern for external customers; showing the same
   detail to internal, non-customer-facing users carries no distinct risk worth
   considering.

### Q2.15 · Claude Application Design
A newly launched Claude-powered feature has no per-user request limits at the
application layer — any single user (or a compromised/scripted account) can submit
unlimited requests, and a runaway client-side bug in one user's session recently drove
the day's entire Claude spend before anyone noticed.

A) This is acceptable, since account-level trust should be sufficient without any
   additional application-layer controls.
B) Per-user (or per-account) rate limiting and quotas at the application layer are a
   reasonable design control here — bounding how much any single user or client can
   consume protects against both abuse and accidental runaway usage, independent of
   whatever limits exist at the API-account level overall.
C) The fix is exclusively to lower the model tier for all users, since a cheaper model
   makes any volume of requests proportionally less costly regardless of actual usage
   patterns.
D) Rate limiting is solely a security concern and has no legitimate place in
   application design decisions about cost and reliability.

### Q2.16 · Claude Application Design
A team is deciding whether a chat application should be fully stateless server-side
(the client resends the entire relevant conversation history with every request) or
should maintain session state server-side (the server tracks conversation history
and the client only sends the latest message plus a session identifier).

A) The stateless, client-resends-everything approach is always correct regardless of
   application needs, since the underlying Messages API itself is stateless.
B) Either design can be appropriate depending on the application's needs: a
   server-side session adds server complexity (state storage, session lifecycle) but
   simplifies the client and can enable features like cross-device continuity; a
   fully client-driven approach keeps the server simple but pushes history management
   and payload size onto the client — the API's own statelessness doesn't dictate which
   the application layer should choose.
C) Server-side session state is impossible to build on top of a stateless API, so the
   client-resends-everything approach is the only option that can work at all.
D) This decision only affects visual design, not application architecture, so either
   choice is functionally interchangeable from an engineering standpoint.

### Q2.17 · Claude Application Design
A production feature currently has no fallback behavior: if the Claude API call fails
or times out for any reason, the user simply sees a blank error page with no
alternative path forward, even though a slightly stale cached response or a
simpler rule-based fallback could often still provide a reasonable answer.

A) This is acceptable, since any failure of the primary call should always be
   surfaced as a hard failure to the user rather than papering over it with a
   fallback.
B) Designing for graceful degradation — a cached or simplified fallback response when
   the primary call fails, rather than a hard failure with no alternative — improves
   the experience for the subset of failures where a reasonable fallback is genuinely
   available, without pretending every failure is silently fine.
C) The fix is exclusively to retry the exact same failing call indefinitely until it
   eventually succeeds, since retries alone are always sufficient to avoid ever
   needing a fallback path.
D) Fallback behavior is unnecessary as long as the underlying API has high uptime,
   since a sufficiently reliable API makes any client-side fallback strategy
   redundant.

### Q2.18 · Configuration Management
A team wants to change a high-traffic production prompt and is deciding between
flipping 100% of traffic to the new version immediately after passing the eval suite,
versus rolling it out to a small percentage of traffic first and expanding gradually
while monitoring real production outcomes.

A) Flipping 100% of traffic immediately is preferable whenever the eval suite passes,
   since a passing eval suite is a complete guarantee that production behavior will
   match expectations at full traffic.
B) A staged rollout (small percentage first, expanding as real production signals
   confirm the change is behaving as expected) reduces the blast radius of an issue an
   eval suite didn't happen to catch, compared to an immediate full cutover — the eval
   suite and a staged rollout are complementary safeguards, not substitutes for each
   other.
C) Staged rollouts are unnecessary for prompt changes specifically, since prompt
   changes carry no more production risk than any other configuration change and can
   always be cut over instantly.
D) The correct approach is to never roll out any prompt change to production traffic
   at all, testing exclusively in a non-production environment indefinitely.

### Q2.19 · Configuration Management *(select TWO)*
Which two of the following are genuine configuration-management practices, as
distinct from good ideas that don't actually belong under that heading?

A) Maintain environment parity (dev/staging/production configured as consistently as
