## Domain 3: Claude Code (Q3.1–Q3.2)

### Q3.1 · Claude Code Operation
A monorepo has a `services/payments/` subdirectory with stricter conventions
(mandatory two-person review notes, no direct legacy SDK calls) than the rest of the
repo. These rules currently live as one more paragraph in the repo-root `CLAUDE.md`,
and engineers report Claude Code frequently doesn't apply them consistently when
working purely inside `services/payments/`.

A) Move the `services/payments/`-specific rules into their own `CLAUDE.md` (or
   `.claude/rules/` file) located inside `services/payments/` itself, so it loads
   specifically when work happens in that directory rather than competing for
   attention as one paragraph in a repo-wide file.
B) Reword the root `CLAUDE.md` paragraph to be more emphatic about the
   payments-specific rules, so the model weighs it more heavily regardless of which
   directory is actually being worked in.
C) Convert the payments conventions into a custom slash command the engineer must
   remember to manually invoke every time they touch a file in that directory.
D) The inconsistency is unrelated to configuration scope and can only be fixed by
   upgrading to a larger model tier.

### Q3.2 · Claude Code Operation
A team wants a specific formatter command to run automatically after every file edit
Claude Code makes in a given repo, with no dependency on the model remembering to run
it or mentioning it in its summary.

A) Add a general reminder in `CLAUDE.md` asking Claude to "always run the formatter
   after editing a file," relying on the model to follow the instruction consistently.
B) Configure a hook (e.g., a `PostToolUse` hook tied to file-edit events) that runs the
   formatter deterministically after every edit, independent of whether the model
   remembers or chooses to mention doing so.
C) Ask the engineer to manually run the formatter after every session, since Claude
   Code has no mechanism for triggering external commands automatically.
D) Rename the formatter binary to something Claude is more likely to reference in its
   own summaries, increasing the odds it gets invoked.

