## Domain 4: Eval, Testing, and Debugging (Q4.1)

### Q4.1 · Debugging and Error Handling
A team makes a prompt change intended to reduce a specific class of formatting error.
They have no eval suite or baseline measurement from before the change, so a week
later, when someone asks "did that change actually help," nobody can answer with
anything more concrete than a general impression that things "feel about the same."

A) This is an acceptable way to work, since prompt changes are inherently
   impossible to measure with any more rigor than general impression.
B) Establishing a baseline eval (a fixed, representative set of test cases, scored
   consistently) before making a change is what makes "did this help" answerable
   afterward — without a baseline, there's no way to distinguish a real improvement, a
   real regression, and no change at all.
C) The correct fix now is exclusively to analyze traces of recent failures to diagnose
   the issue, since trace analysis alone can substitute for having had a baseline in
   the first place.
D) Since the change was intended to reduce a specific class of error, its intent alone
   is sufficient evidence that it worked, regardless of any measurement.

