## Domain 5: Model Selection and Optimization (Q5.1–Q5.10)

### Q5.1 · LLM Fundamentals
A team conflates `temperature` and `top_p`, treating them as two names for the exact
same sampling control, and sets both to unusual extreme values simultaneously assuming
this doubles the effect of "more randomness."

A) `temperature` and `top_p` are literally identical mechanisms, so setting both
   simultaneously is the correct way to intensify the same single effect.
B) `temperature` and `top_p` are distinct sampling controls that interact rather than
   duplicate each other; the team should understand what each one actually does
   before tuning both simultaneously, rather than assuming stacking them straightforwardly
   compounds "more randomness" in a simple, additive way.
C) `top_p` is deprecated in current models and has no effect regardless of its value,
   so only `temperature` needs to be considered.
D) Combining any two sampling parameters at extreme values is always safe and
   predictable, since sampling parameters never interact with each other.

### Q5.2 · LLM Fundamentals
A team builds an application assuming every Claude model tier shares an identical
200,000-token context window, and is surprised when a request that fit comfortably on
one tier is rejected as too large on a different, smaller-context tier they later
switch to for cost reasons.

A) This is a documentation error on Anthropic's part, since context window size should
   be identical across all tiers by default.
B) Context window size can differ across model families and tiers, so an application
   that assumes a single fixed window size regardless of which model it's actually
   calling should verify the specific model's documented context window rather than
   carrying an assumption over from a different tier.
C) Context window size is determined solely by the API key's account tier, not by
   which specific model is called, so switching models should never have affected
   this.
D) The rejection must indicate the request also exceeded `max_tokens`, since context
   window size cannot itself be the actual cause of a request being rejected as too
   large.

### Q5.3 · LLM Fundamentals
A multi-shot prompt includes five examples for a classification task, with the four
easiest, most typical examples listed first and the one genuinely ambiguous edge-case
example listed last, right before the actual input to classify. The model
under-classifies ambiguous real inputs into the easy, typical categories.

A) Example order has no bearing on how a multi-shot prompt is interpreted, so this
   pattern couldn't be contributing to the observed under-classification.
B) Reconsidering how examples are ordered and balanced (e.g., ensuring the ambiguous
   case isn't the sole edge-case example, and that a few clear examples don't overwhelm
   the prompt's implicit signal about how common each category actually is) is worth
   testing here, since example composition and positioning can shape what a model
   infers about the task's typical distribution.
C) The fix is to remove all five examples and rely on zero-shot prompting exclusively,
   since any multi-shot prompt with more than three examples is inherently unreliable.
D) The fix is to increase the number of easy, typical examples even further, since more
   examples of the common case should reduce ambiguity for the model.

### Q5.4 · Technical Fundamentals
A service catches every exception from Claude API calls with one broad
`except Exception` block, logging a generic "something went wrong" message regardless
of whether the actual cause was a rate limit, an authentication failure, or a
malformed request — making it impossible to apply the right recovery strategy per
error type without manually inspecting each raw log.

A) One broad exception handler is sufficient for any integration, since all failures
   ultimately require the same generic response of logging and retrying.
B) Catching and handling the SDK's typed error classes distinctly (e.g., a rate-limit
   error, an authentication error, a malformed-request error) lets the service apply
   the right recovery strategy per error type — retry-with-backoff for one, alerting
   for another, a code fix for a third — instead of collapsing genuinely different
   failures into one generic response.
C) The fix is to stop using the SDK's exception types entirely and parse raw HTTP
   status codes by hand, since SDK exceptions provide no more information than a
   status code would.
D) Exception handling granularity has no bearing on recovery strategy, since recovery
   strategy should be identical regardless of what specifically caused a given
   failure.

### Q5.5 · Technical Fundamentals
A team builds its own custom thread-pool-based concurrency manager to parallelize API
calls, duplicating rate-limit-aware throttling and connection-pooling logic that an
official SDK's built-in async client and concurrency controls already provide,
reasoning "our custom logic gives us more control."

A) Custom concurrency logic is always preferable to whatever an SDK provides, since
   full control is inherently more valuable than convenience regardless of what's
   already available.
B) Where the official SDK already provides concurrency controls suited to the actual
   need (bounded parallelism, rate-limit awareness), re-implementing equivalent logic
   by hand typically costs more to build and maintain correctly than it's worth —
   custom concurrency logic earns its cost when the SDK's built-in controls genuinely
   don't fit the specific need.
C) SDKs never provide any concurrency-related functionality, so building custom
   concurrency logic is the only way to parallelize calls at all.
D) The choice between custom and SDK-provided concurrency logic has no bearing on
   correctness, only on code length.

### Q5.6 · Technical Fundamentals
A client needs Claude's response streamed to the browser as it's generated, with no
need for the browser to push anything back to the server mid-response beyond the
original request. An engineer proposes a full bidirectional websocket connection for
this specific need.

A) A websocket is the only mechanism capable of delivering streamed content to a
   browser under any circumstances, so it's the correct and only choice here
   regardless of directionality needs.
B) For a case needing only server-to-client streaming with no mid-response
   client-to-server push, a simpler one-directional streaming mechanism (such as
   server-sent events) fits the actual need without the added complexity of a full
   bidirectional websocket connection, which earns its cost when genuine
   bidirectional, mid-stream communication is actually required.
C) Websockets and server-sent events are functionally identical in every respect, so
   the choice between them has no practical consequence for this or any other use
   case.
D) Streaming to a browser is impossible without a websocket connection, since HTTP
   responses cannot be delivered incrementally under any other mechanism.

### Q5.7 · Model Selection and Tradeoffs
A high-volume classification workload sends every request to the most capable (and
most expensive) model tier, even though the large majority of inputs are
straightforward and only a small fraction are genuinely ambiguous enough to benefit
from the strongest available reasoning.

A) This is the correct approach, since customer-facing accuracy always justifies using
   the most capable tier for every request regardless of how straightforward most
   inputs actually are.
B) A cascading approach — routing the bulk of straightforward inputs to a faster,
   cheaper tier and escalating only the inputs a lighter-weight check flags as
   genuinely ambiguous to a stronger tier — can meaningfully reduce cost while
   preserving accuracy where it actually matters, rather than paying top-tier cost
   uniformly regardless of input difficulty.
C) The correct approach is to route every request to the cheapest tier uniformly,
   since cost should always be minimized regardless of any accuracy tradeoff.
