   always produces inconsistent results too unpredictable to be worth the cost
   savings.

### Q5.8 · Model Selection and Tradeoffs
A team wants to decide between two model tiers for a live production workload and is
choosing between trusting only offline benchmark comparisons versus running a live
production A/B test that splits real traffic between the two tiers and measures actual
outcomes (user satisfaction, task success, cost) before fully committing to one.

A) Offline benchmark comparisons alone are always sufficient for this decision, since
   published benchmark scores are guaranteed to predict real production outcomes for
   any given application.
B) A live A/B test on real production traffic captures how each tier actually performs
   on the application's specific distribution of real inputs and real user outcomes,
   which offline benchmarks (run on a different, generic distribution) can't fully
   substitute for — the two approaches are complementary, not interchangeable.
C) A/B testing model tiers in production is never advisable under any circumstances,
   since any live experiment on production traffic is inherently too risky to justify.
D) The choice between tiers should be made randomly, since neither offline benchmarks
   nor live A/B testing meaningfully differ in what they reveal about tier performance.

### Q5.9 · Cost and Token Management
A low-volume internal tool makes only a handful of requests per day, each with a large
static system prompt. The team enables prompt caching expecting significant savings,
but per-request cost barely changes, since the small number of daily requests rarely
reuses the cache before it expires, while a similarly-large-prompt, high-volume
customer-facing feature elsewhere in the company sees caching deliver its expected
savings.

A) Caching should deliver identical savings regardless of request volume, so the
   low-volume tool's result must indicate a configuration error rather than a genuine
   difference in reuse pattern.
B) Caching's benefit is tied to how often the cached prefix is actually reused before
   it expires; a low-volume workload that rarely re-hits the cache before expiry won't
   realize the same savings a high-volume, frequently-reused workload does — for the
   low-volume tool, other levers (trimming the static prompt, a smaller model tier)
   may matter more than caching.
C) The fix for the low-volume tool is to disable caching and switch exclusively to a
   larger model tier, since caching provides no benefit whatsoever for internal tools
   specifically.
D) Caching savings depend only on prompt size, not on how frequently requests occur, so
   request volume should have had no bearing on the outcome observed here.

### Q5.10 · LLM Fundamentals
A team assumes adaptive thinking and the `effort` parameter are only relevant to
tasks the team considers "hard" and manually disables thinking for every request they
've judged as simple in advance, without ever letting the model itself assess
per-request whether deeper reasoning would help a specific input within that
"simple" category.

A) This is optimal, since a human's upfront judgment of task difficulty is always more
