## Domain 6: Prompt and Context Engineering (Q6.1–Q6.7)

### Q6.1 · Context Engineering
A support agent has access to a large internal knowledge base. The current design
preloads the entire knowledge base into context at the start of every conversation,
regardless of what the actual question turns out to be, crowding out room for the
conversation itself on lengthy interactions.

A) Preloading everything up front is the safest design, since it guarantees the
   relevant content is always present regardless of what's eventually asked.
B) A just-in-time context pattern — retrieving only the specific knowledge-base
   content relevant to the actual question, at the point it's needed, rather than
   preloading the entire base regardless of relevance — uses context budget far more
   efficiently for a knowledge base too large to fit comfortably alongside a lengthy
   conversation.
C) The fix is to reduce the size of the knowledge base itself so it always fits
   alongside any conversation length, discarding content instead of retrieving it
   selectively.
D) Preloading versus just-in-time retrieval has no bearing on context budget, since
   context window limits only apply to the conversation itself, not to any content
   supplied at the start of a session.

### Q6.2 · Context Engineering
A team's harness appends every tool result directly into the system prompt so it
"stays available," rather than passing it as part of the ordinary turn-by-turn
message history, resulting in a system prompt that grows across a session and a
distinction between "standing instructions" and "this turn's tool output" that has
effectively disappeared.

A) This is a reasonable design, since anything added to the system prompt is
   automatically prioritized more highly by the model regardless of its actual nature.
B) Tool results are turn-specific data and belong in the conversational message
   history, not folded permanently into the system prompt; conflating standing,
   session-level instructions with turn-specific data undermines the distinct roles
   each is meant to play and makes the system prompt an ever-growing, undifferentiated
   blob.
C) The fix is to stop using tool results in context at all, since any tool output that
   isn't in the system prompt is inaccessible to the model.
D) System prompt content and message-history content are functionally identical in
   every respect, so where a given piece of content is placed has no consequence.

### Q6.3 · Prompt Engineering
A team adds "act as a world-renowned expert in this field" role-framing to a prompt
for a fully deterministic, rule-based classification task (mapping an exact input
string to one of three fixed categories via clear, unambiguous criteria), expecting
the framing to meaningfully improve accuracy.

A) Role-framing meaningfully improves any task regardless of its nature, so applying
   it here is a reliable way to improve classification accuracy.
B) Role-framing tends to help most on tasks that benefit from a particular
   perspective, tone, or style of reasoning; a fully deterministic, criteria-based
   classification task doesn't have that kind of room for a persona to add value, so
   the framing here is more likely to be inert noise than a meaningful accuracy lever
   — clear criteria and examples are what actually drive performance on this kind of
   task.
C) Role-framing should never be used under any circumstances, since it provides no
   benefit for any task type.
D) The fix is to combine role-framing with a significantly higher temperature, since
   the two techniques compound each other's effectiveness on any task.

### Q6.4 · Prompt Engineering
An application builds prompts by directly string-concatenating raw user-supplied
values (name, order number, freeform comment) into a hand-written prompt template
with no structured variable substitution or escaping, making it easy for an unusual
user-supplied value to accidentally break the template's intended structure or blend
into the surrounding instructions.

A) This is fine as long as the model is generally capable, since a capable model will
   always correctly parse the intended structure regardless of how the prompt is
   assembled.
B) Using structured, well-defined variable substitution (with clear boundaries around
   inserted values) rather than ad hoc string concatenation reduces the risk that an
   unusual user-supplied value distorts the prompt's intended structure or gets
   confused with the surrounding instructions — a discipline related to, but distinct
   from, sanitizing content specifically to prevent instruction-following on embedded
   text.
C) The fix is to reject any user-supplied value containing punctuation, since
   punctuation is the only category of input that could ever disrupt a template.
D) Prompt template design has no bearing on reliability, since any two prompts
   containing the same underlying information will always produce equivalent results
   regardless of how they're assembled.

### Q6.5 · Prompt Engineering
A team manually instructs the model with "think through this step by step before
answering" in the prompt text for a workload running on a model generation that
supports adaptive thinking, reasoning that explicit chain-of-thought instructions and
the built-in thinking feature are two separate techniques that should both always be
used together for every request regardless of task.

A) Explicit "think step by step" prompt instructions and the model's own adaptive
   thinking are not simply additive; on a model generation where adaptive thinking is
   available, letting the model decide when and how much to reason (optionally guided
   by the `effort` parameter) is generally the more direct lever, while manual
   chain-of-thought phrasing was more essential on models or contexts lacking that
   capability — combining both by default on every request isn't automatically better
   than using the mechanism actually suited to the situation.
B) The two techniques are functionally identical and redundant in every case, so using
   both together produces no different result than using either alone.
C) Explicit chain-of-thought instructions should always be removed entirely once
   adaptive thinking is available, since they become actively harmful to output
   quality in every single case.
D) Adaptive thinking replaces the need for well-structured prompts entirely, so prompt
   quality no longer matters at all once thinking is enabled.

### Q6.6 · Output Handling
A pipeline needs a downstream system to receive one complete, valid JSON object from a
tool call. The current implementation parses the streaming response as JSON fragments
arrive incrementally, attempting to act on partial, syntactically incomplete JSON
before the relevant content block has finished streaming, occasionally passing
malformed data downstream.

A) This is unavoidable, since JSON output can never be reliably combined with
   streaming under any implementation approach.
B) Buffer the streamed content until the relevant block signals completion, then parse
   the fully assembled JSON as a single complete unit before passing it downstream —
   acting on partial fragments trades a small latency saving for a real risk of
   passing invalid, unusable data forward.
C) The fix is to disable JSON output entirely and switch the tool to return plain text
   instead, since plain text is inherently compatible with incremental parsing in a
   way JSON never can be.
D) The fix is to increase the model's `max_tokens`, since malformed partial JSON is
   typically a symptom of an output-length limit rather than of parsing an
   in-progress stream too early.

