## Domain 7: Security and Safety (Q7.1–Q7.5)

### Q7.1 · AI Application Security
An agent has a `fetch_url` tool that retrieves the content of any URL supplied in
freeform text, with no restriction on which destinations are reachable. A crafted
input gets the agent to fetch an internal-only administrative endpoint that was never
meant to be reachable from outside the internal network, using the agent's own network
position as a bridge to reach it.

A) This is expected and not a genuine risk, since the agent is only following a URL it
   was given rather than accessing anything on its own initiative.
B) This is a server-side request forgery-style risk: a tool that can fetch arbitrary,
   caller-supplied destinations should have its reachable destinations scoped (an
   allowlist of permitted domains/endpoints, or network-level restrictions) rather
   than being able to reach anything the underlying network path allows, regardless of
   whether the destination was ever intended to be reachable this way.
C) The fix is exclusively to lower the model's temperature, since a lower temperature
   would make the model less likely to comply with a crafted instruction to fetch an
   unusual URL.
D) The fix is to remove all tools capable of any network access whatsoever, since no
   scoping mechanism can ever meaningfully restrict what a fetch-style tool can reach.

### Q7.2 · AI Application Security
A team uses a single Anthropic API key across development, staging, and production
environments, reasoning "it's simpler to manage one key." A bug introduced during
local development testing ends up making calls against the same key used by the live
production application, muddying production usage metrics and, in one incident,
consuming enough of a shared rate limit to degrade production response times.

A) This is acceptable, since using one key everywhere is always operationally simpler
   regardless of the cross-environment risk it introduces.
B) Environment-level separation (distinct keys per environment, ideally with distinct
   rate-limit and spend visibility per key) prevents a development-time bug from
   directly affecting production traffic and usage accounting, rather than sharing
   fate across environments that should be isolated from each other.
C) This is purely a cost-tracking inconvenience with no bearing on production
   reliability, so it doesn't warrant separate keys per environment.
D) The fix is to rotate the single shared key more frequently, which fully resolves
   the cross-environment interference regardless of how many environments share it.

### Q7.3 · Guardrails and Safe Deployment
A medical-information chatbot's only safeguard against providing specific diagnostic
or treatment recommendations (which the company's policy prohibits, requiring
referral to a licensed professional instead) is a single system-prompt line: "avoid
giving specific medical advice."

A) This is sufficient, since a clearly worded instruction reliably and completely
   prevents this category of output on its own.
B) As with any compliance-sensitive domain, a single prompt-level instruction is one
   probabilistic layer; a genuinely safe posture in a domain like medical guidance
   typically layers output-side checks (scanning for diagnostic/treatment-recommendation
   patterns before a response reaches the user) and human escalation paths on top of —
   not instead of — the prompt-level guidance.
C) This is unnecessary, since compliance requirements around medical guidance don't
   meaningfully apply to AI-generated conversational responses.
D) The fix is exclusively to increase the model's context window, since a larger
   window makes the model less likely to produce prohibited content.

### Q7.4 · Claude Hooks
A coding agent occasionally reads or edits files inside a `.env` file and a `secrets/`
directory that should never be touched by an automated process, and the team's only
current safeguard is a system-prompt line asking the agent to "avoid reading
sensitive configuration files."

A) This is adequate, since a clearly worded instruction reliably prevents access to
   specifically named sensitive paths.
B) A hook that deterministically blocks read/edit operations on specific
   paths/patterns (e.g., `.env`, `secrets/`) before they execute is the appropriate
   enforcement mechanism — path-based access restriction on genuinely sensitive
   locations shouldn't depend on the model's own judgment holding in every
   circumstance.
C) The fix is to delete the `.env` file and `secrets/` directory from the repository
   entirely, removing the credentials the agent might otherwise access rather than
   restricting access to them.
D) The fix is to increase the model's temperature, since more varied outputs make the
   agent less likely to systematically target the same sensitive paths.

### Q7.5 · Identity, Secrets, and Key Management
A team has never rotated any of its API keys or service credentials since initial
setup, reasoning "if there's no evidence of a breach, there's no reason to rotate."

A) This reasoning is sound; credential rotation is only ever warranted reactively,
   after a specific, confirmed exposure incident, and has no value as a standing
   practice otherwise.
B) Periodic credential rotation as a standing hygiene practice — independent of
   whether a breach has been detected — limits the exposure window of a credential
   that may have been compromised without detection (via an unnoticed leak, a former
   employee's continued access, or a log inadvertently capturing it), rather than
   relying solely on reactive rotation after a confirmed incident.
C) Rotation should happen constantly, on every single request, since any rotation
   cadence slower than per-request leaves a meaningful exposure window.
D) Rotation cadence is irrelevant as long as credentials are stored in a secrets
   manager, since storage location alone fully determines exposure risk regardless of
   how long a given credential remains valid.

