## Domain 8: Tools and MCPs (Q8.1–Q8.7)

### Q8.1 · Tool Implementation
A `get_document` tool always returns a document's full raw text in its result,
regardless of how long the document is or how much of it the current task actually
needs, causing a 50-page document to flood the agent's context on every single call
even when only one short section is relevant.

A) This is fine, since returning the complete, unmodified document is always the
   safest choice regardless of how much of it the current task actually needs.
B) Tool output design should return concise, relevant content rather than a full raw
   payload by default — supporting pagination, section retrieval, or a
   summarized/excerpted view lets the tool serve the cases that need a small piece
   without forcing every call to pay the context cost of the entire document.
C) The fix is to remove the tool entirely and have the agent request document content
   through the system prompt instead, since tools should never return large content.
D) The fix is to increase the model's context window so it can always accommodate a
   full document regardless of how much of it any given task actually needs.

### Q8.2 · Tool Implementation
An agent has over 200 tools available simultaneously in every session, most of them
irrelevant to any single given task, and tool-selection accuracy has degraded
noticeably as the toolset has grown, with the agent occasionally choosing a
plausible-sounding but wrong tool from among many similar options.

A) This is expected and cannot be improved; tool-selection accuracy is entirely
   independent of how many tools are available in a given session regardless of scale.
B) Curating which tools are actually exposed for a given task or session — rather than
   presenting the full 200-tool catalog uniformly regardless of relevance — reduces the
   selection burden and the chance of a plausible-but-wrong choice among many similar
   options; this can be done through task-scoped toolsets, grouping, or a routing
   step that narrows the visible set before the agent has to choose.
C) The fix is exclusively to rewrite every one of the 200 tools' descriptions to be
   longer and more detailed, since description length alone determines selection
   accuracy regardless of how many tools are visible at once.
D) The fix is to increase temperature so the agent explores tool choices more broadly
   before settling on one.

### Q8.3 · Tool Implementation
A `get_order_details` tool's schema is updated to add a new optional `loyalty_tier`
field, and the team wants existing agent behavior and existing callers to continue
working exactly as before, without requiring every consumer to be updated in lockstep
with the schema change.

A) Any schema change, no matter how small, requires every consumer to be updated in
   lockstep before it can be deployed, since tool schemas cannot evolve in a
   backward-compatible way under any circumstances.
B) Adding a new field as optional, rather than required, preserves backward
   compatibility — existing callers and existing agent behavior that never reference
   the new field continue to work exactly as before, while new callers can opt into
   using it once they're updated.
C) The fix is to create an entirely new, separately-named tool for every schema change,
   however small, rather than ever evolving an existing tool's schema.
D) Schema versioning has no bearing on tool behavior, since the model always adapts
   automatically and correctly to any schema change with zero risk of disruption.

