## Answer Key & Rationale — Set 3

### Domain 1

**Q1.1 — B.** A hybrid handles the fully-specified majority with a fixed sequence and
routes the genuinely open-ended multi-city case to agentic reasoning — matching each
part of the task to what it actually needs. *(A discards real requests instead of
handling them; C pays full autonomy overhead on the simple majority; D invents
per-destination subagent structure a simple branch doesn't require.)*

**Q1.2 — B.** Classifying and routing to the specialist(s) a request actually concerns
is the entire point of having specialists — forwarding everything through one by
default defeats it. *(A treats blanket routing as thoroughness; C optimizes for the
wrong variable (speed) instead of relevance; D misassigns a routing-logic problem to
hooks.)*

**Q1.3 — B.** The simple lookup doesn't need isolation; the open-ended, potentially
many-attempt reproduction does — matching delegation to actual need for expendable,
exploratory context. *(A and C treat delegation as governed by tool count rather than
task shape; D wrongly restricts subagents to cross-domain work only.)*

**Q1.4 — B.** Budget and turn caps guard against different failure modes; a cheap,
high-turn-count loop needs `max_turns` specifically, since it may never trip a dollar
threshold. *(A accepts an unbounded loop as harmless because it's individually cheap;
C and D each apply the wrong lever to a problem that isn't about per-turn cost or
length.)*

**Q1.5 — A.** Fork the risky, isolated exploration; the main thread continues
unaffected precisely because it was never forked away from. *(B misapplies resume,
which restores a saved state rather than creating a parallel isolated branch; C
ignores a real need for isolation; D forks the wrong side of the scenario.)*

**Q1.6 — B.** Elastic, rare-peak demand with no desire to manage capacity planning is
exactly the tradeoff managed hosting is built for. *(A commits to permanent
infrastructure sized for a rare case; C and D each deny or overstate a limitation that
doesn't hold.)*

**Q1.7 — A.** Match framework structure to actual correctness need — strict typed
validation for the task where a bad intermediate value truly matters, none of that
overhead for the simple task that doesn't have the same failure mode. *(B and D apply
structure regardless of fit; C denies a framework can meaningfully help at all.)*

**Q1.8 — B.** Case-scoped (not session- or even user-scoped) persistent memory is what
lets any session, any agent, pick the case back up coherently. *(A relies on a
lifetime the scenario shows is too short; C scopes to the wrong entity when a case can
span more than the interactions of one person; D substitutes window size for actual
persistence.)*

**Q1.9 — A and C.** Specialization genuinely helps multi-domain routing, and
coordination/aggregation genuinely costs something a single agent doesn't pay — both
real, non-absolute tradeoffs. *(B and D assert guarantees — zero overhead, zero
misrouting — that don't hold; E is the second correct statement, not a distractor:
aggregation introduces its own failure surface.)*

### Domain 2

**Q2.1 — B.** A specific calculation is functional; a DR/availability target
regardless of feature is infrastructure — two genuinely distinct categories here. *(A
and D collapse the distinction; C misclassifies a statable, testable constraint as a
mere business goal.)*

**Q2.2 — B.** Without a measurable target, "quickly" and "snappy" can't be verified
against — the requirement needs clarification into something testable before
implementation proceeds. *(A treats vague language as sufficient; C mislabels an
unrefined statement as already infrastructure-grade; D overcorrects by discarding a
salvageable requirement.)*

**Q2.3 — B.** Turning a completed design into working code and its accompanying unit
tests is the defining implementation activity. *(A conflates design with its
execution; C and D each misplace this activity into an earlier or unrelated phase.)*

**Q2.4 — B.** Verifying the finished build against expectations before real traffic
sees it is a distinct testing/validation activity, separate from both implementation
and post-launch operations. *(A misplaces a pre-launch activity into ongoing
operations; C conflates fixing found issues with implementation itself; D wrongly
treats one eval run as sufficient regardless of what UAT is meant to add.)*

**Q2.5 — A.** Multiple image content blocks are supported per message, and each
contributes to input token cost — the team should size their estimate for all four
together. *(B, C, and D each invent a restriction — single-image-only, zero token
cost, or a thinking-vision conflict — that doesn't hold.)*

**Q2.6 — B.** Thinking tokens are billed as part of output, so a workload doing
substantial internal reasoning will show higher output token counts than the visible
answer alone suggests. *(A misreads an accurate bill as an error; C and D each invent
a flat-fee or effort-gated billing mechanism that isn't how it works.)*

**Q2.7 — B.** Waiting for a content block to finish streaming before parsing and
executing its JSON avoids acting on incomplete, invalid data. *(A and C overcorrect
into declaring tool use and streaming incompatible; D misattributes malformed partial
JSON to an unrelated output-length limit.)*

**Q2.8 — A.** The `system` parameter is the purpose-built mechanism for persistent,
session-level instructions, distinct in role from the turn-by-turn `messages` array —
placement is not interchangeable. *(B and C assert an equivalence or deprecation that
doesn't hold; D invents an API restriction that doesn't exist.)*

**Q2.9 — B.** An idempotency mechanism lets a retried call after a timeout be
recognized as the same operation instead of creating a duplicate. *(A dismisses a real
data-integrity problem; C discards retries entirely instead of making them safe; D
reduces but doesn't eliminate the possibility of a timeout, so it doesn't fix the
underlying unsafe-retry issue.)*

**Q2.10 — B.** Structured, contextual logging is what makes reconstructing a specific
failure possible at all — sparse generic logs can't support that. *(A settles for
category-level tracking when instance-level tracing is the actual need; C and D each
substitute an unrelated fix — model capability or evals — for the missing
observability itself.)*

**Q2.11 — B.** Fast, cheap unit tests for logic that doesn't need a live call, reserved
end-to-end tests for what genuinely requires the real integration, catches more
regressions faster and cheaper than either extreme alone. *(A and C each treat one
test type as sufficient on its own; D wrongly claims LLM-integrated code needs no
adjustment to general testing practice.)*

**Q2.12 — B.** Version-pinning the SDK and treating upgrades as deliberate, tested
changes prevents an unannounced default-behavior change from silently altering
production output. *(A treats automatic adoption of unreviewed changes as inherently
beneficial; C misassigns an SDK-level concern to model versioning; D discards a
working tool instead of managing its version properly.)*

**Q2.13 — B.** Structuring or modularizing a long prompt makes isolated updates safer
to reason about, even though the final assembled prompt can still be sent as one
piece of text. *(A treats structurelessness as inherent to the mechanism itself; C
removes legitimately necessary content instead of organizing it; D invents an API
restriction that doesn't exist.)*

**Q2.14 — B.** Separating what a customer needs (clear, actionable messaging) from
what engineering needs (full internal detail, kept in logs) is standard practice for
user-facing error handling. *(A treats raw internal detail as always more helpful than
a designed message; C removes even the minimal signal a user needs; D wrongly assumes
internal audiences carry no distinct exposure consideration.)*

**Q2.15 — B.** Per-user/per-account limits at the application layer bound both abuse
and accidental runaway usage, independent of overall account-level API limits. *(A
assumes trust substitutes for a technical control; C addresses per-unit cost without
addressing unbounded volume; D wrongly restricts rate limiting to a security-only
concern.)*

**Q2.16 — B.** Both stateless-client and server-session designs are legitimate,
application-level tradeoffs — the API's own statelessness doesn't force one choice.
*(A and C each treat the API's mechanics as dictating the application's architecture
when it doesn't; D understates real architectural consequences of the choice.)*

**Q2.17 — B.** A cached or simplified fallback improves outcomes for the failures
where one is genuinely available, without claiming every failure can be silently
smoothed over. *(A treats every failure as needing to be a hard stop; C substitutes
indefinite retrying for having any fallback path at all; D assumes high uptime removes
the value of degrading gracefully on the failures that still occur.)*

**Q2.18 — B.** A staged rollout reduces blast radius for whatever an eval suite didn't
happen to catch — the two safeguards complement rather than substitute for each
other. *(A treats a passing eval suite as a complete guarantee; C understates real
prompt-change risk; D overcorrects into never shipping changes to production at
all.)*

**Q2.19 — A and B.** Environment parity and feature-flagged variant control are both
genuine, auditable configuration-management practices. *(C is a security failure
disguised as convenience; D and E each remove the documentation or safety net
configuration management exists to provide.)*

### Domain 3

**Q3.1 — A.** Directory-scoped configuration loads specifically when work happens in
that directory, rather than competing for attention as one paragraph in a repo-wide
file. *(B is the same location, just louder wording; C reintroduces a manual-recall
step the model shouldn't need; D misattributes a scoping problem to model capability.)*

**Q3.2 — B.** A hook fires deterministically on the file-edit event itself, independent
of whether the model remembers or chooses to mention running the formatter. *(A and D
rely on the model's own judgment or naming choices holding reliably; C denies a real
mechanism Claude Code actually has.)*

### Domain 4

**Q4.1 — B.** A baseline eval taken before the change is what makes "did this help"
answerable afterward — without one, before/after comparison has nothing to compare
against. *(A accepts unmeasurable change as normal; C substitutes trace analysis for a
baseline it can't retroactively create; D treats stated intent as equivalent to
verified outcome.)*

### Domain 5

**Q5.1 — B.** `temperature` and `top_p` are distinct, interacting controls, not
duplicate levers for one effect — understanding each is a prerequisite to tuning both.
*(A and C each assert an equivalence or deprecation that doesn't hold; D wrongly
claims parameter interaction never occurs.)*

**Q5.2 — B.** Context window size can genuinely differ by model, so an assumption
carried over from a different tier should be checked against that specific model's
documentation, not assumed. *(A and C each misattribute the cause to a documentation
error or an unrelated account-level property; D deflects from context window as the
actual, stated cause.)*

**Q5.3 — B.** Example ordering and balance can shape what a model infers about a
task's typical distribution — worth reconsidering when ambiguous cases are
under-classified into heavily-represented easy categories. *(A denies a real,
testable effect; C and D each overcorrect toward removing examples entirely or adding
more of the wrong kind.)*

**Q5.4 — B.** Handling the SDK's typed error classes distinctly enables the right
recovery strategy per actual cause, instead of collapsing genuinely different
failures into one generic response. *(A and D treat all failures as equivalent when
they aren't; C discards information the SDK already correctly provides.)*

**Q5.5 — B.** Where the SDK's built-in concurrency controls already fit the need,
re-implementing them by hand typically costs more to build and maintain correctly than
it saves. *(A and C each overstate custom logic's necessity or deny the SDK offers
this at all; D denies a real correctness consequence of getting concurrency handling
