**Q5.6 — B.** A one-directional streaming mechanism fits a need with no
mid-response client-to-server push, without paying for bidirectional complexity that
isn't needed. *(A and D overstate websockets as the only option; C denies a real,
consequential difference between the two mechanisms.)*

**Q5.7 — B.** Cascading routes the bulk of easy inputs to a cheaper tier and reserves
the expensive tier for inputs actually flagged as ambiguous, cutting cost without
sacrificing accuracy where it matters. *(A pays top-tier cost uniformly regardless of
need; C sacrifices accuracy uniformly instead of selectively; D dismisses a real,
usable strategy as inherently unworkable.)*

**Q5.8 — B.** A live A/B test captures real-world performance on the application's
actual input distribution and outcomes in a way generic offline benchmarks can't fully
substitute for — the two approaches complement each other. *(A and D overstate
benchmark sufficiency or deny any meaningful difference; C dismisses a standard,
valuable practice as categorically too risky.)*

**Q5.9 — B.** Caching's payoff scales with how often the prefix is actually reused
before expiring — a low-volume workload simply doesn't generate the reuse a
